  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout warn">EVEN MORE SPECIALIZED THAN BANKING: insurance accounting adds a genuine ACTUARIAL dimension on top of ordinary financial accounting. This poster gives real depth to the confirmed, conceptually central "Versicherungstechnische Rückstellungen" Titel (§§341e–341h), while presenting the surrounding Titel (measurement, group accounts) at an appropriately structural level given their more technical, regulation-cross-referenced detail.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Where accounting meets actuarial science</div>
      <div class="principle">A bank's balance sheet, covered in the previous poster, is dominated by financial instruments whose values are largely observable or model-derived from market data. An insurer's balance sheet is dominated instead by GENUINELY LONG-TERM, uncertain future obligations — the promise to pay claims or benefits potentially decades in the future — requiring an entirely different accounting apparatus rooted in actuarial estimation rather than market measurement. §§341–341p exist to house that apparatus.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§341–341P MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Vorsicht, at its most demanding</strong> — §341e(1)'s own "dauernde Erfüllbarkeit" (permanent fulfillability) standard is arguably the single most demanding application of the Vorsichtsprinzip anywhere in this poster series</span>
        <span><strong>Sector-specific volatility management</strong> — the Schwankungsrückstellung (Section 12) reflects a genuinely distinctive prudential response to a risk profile ordinary corporate accounting never has to address</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">The confirmed Titel structure</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>Titel</span><span>Content</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>1st</span><span>Scope (§341)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>2nd</span><span>Jahresabschluss, Lagebericht (§341a)</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>3rd</span><span>Measurement (§§341b–341d)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>4th</span><span>Technical provisions (§§341e–341h) — this poster's centerpiece</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>5th</span><span>Konzernabschluss, Konzernlagebericht (§§341i–341j)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — §341(1)-(4)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Insurance undertakings, specific exclusions, EU/EEA branches, and pension funds</div>
      <div class="principle">§341(1) applies this Subsection to Versicherungsunternehmen (insurance undertakings) that are not social insurance carriers, with an EXCLUSION for certain mutual/member-benefit arrangements funded on a pay-as-you-go basis — UNLESS organised as an AG, a Versicherungsverein auf Gegenseitigkeit (VVaG, mutual insurance association), or a legally-capable municipal casualty insurer. §341(2) extends coverage to domestic branches of such undertakings. §341(2a) requires EU/EEA insurance branches NOT needing separate German authorisation to apply ONLY the recognition/measurement provisions of this Subsection (a narrower requirement than full compliance). §341(4) extends the FIRST through SEVENTH Titel of this Subsection CORRESPONDINGLY to Pensionsfonds (pension funds, per VAG §236(1)).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§341a — Corresponding Application of the General Framework</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Borrowing the corporate framework by reference, once again</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§341a applies the GENERAL corporate Jahresabschluss/Lagebericht provisions this entire poster series has documented CORRESPONDINGLY to insurance undertakings, subject to this Subsection's own specific adaptations — the SAME "borrowing by reference" structural pattern already seen for §298(1) and §336(2).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>§341b–341d — Investment Measurement and the Unit-Linked Life Insurance Concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Genuinely massive institutional investment portfolios, measured with sector-specific care</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§341b governs the measurement of Vermögensgegenstände (Kapitalanlagen — investments), reflecting insurers' own role as among Germany's largest institutional investors. §341c addresses specific instrument types — Namensschuldverschreibungen (registered debt instruments), Hypothekendarlehen (mortgage loans), and other receivables — commonly used by German insurers as long-duration assets matching their own long-duration liabilities. §341d addresses the "Anlagestock der fondsgebundenen Lebensversicherung" — the dedicated investment pool backing UNIT-LINKED life insurance policies, where policyholders themselves bear the underlying investment risk/return rather than the insurer — requiring this pool to be presented and measured distinctly, reflecting its genuinely different risk allocation from the insurer's own general investment portfolio.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§341e — the General Principle, and a Notable Discounting Divergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">"Dauernde Erfüllbarkeit" — a demanding standard, with an explicit no-discounting rule</div>
      <div class="principle">§341e(1) requires insurance undertakings to form versicherungstechnische Rückstellungen (technical/actuarial provisions) to the extent NECESSARY, per REASONABLE COMMERCIAL JUDGEMENT, to ensure the PERMANENT fulfillability (dauernde Erfüllbarkeit) of obligations arising from insurance contracts — a genuinely demanding prudential standard. Regulatory provisions (calculation bases, interest rates, capital income allocation — EXCEPT VAG §§74–87) must also be observed. CRITICALLY, §341e(1) sentence 3 EXPLICITLY EXCLUDES discounting under §253(2) — the general long-term-provision discounting rule established very early in this series — meaning technical provisions are measured WITHOUT applying that otherwise-general HGB discounting mechanic, reflecting the insurance-specific calculation methodology (itself often embedding its own actuarial discount rate directly within the underlying reserving basis) rather than layering the general corporate discounting rule on top.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Why Technical Provisions Are Never Discounted Under §253(2)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A genuine, explicit carve-out from a rule established very early in this series</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Contrasting an ordinary long-term provision with a technical provision</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An ordinary manufacturing company recognises a long-term warranty provision extending beyond one year, which §253(2) — established in the very early Measurement poster — requires to be DISCOUNTED at the Bundesbank-published rate. A life insurer, meanwhile, recognises its own Deckungsrückstellung, a technical provision extending decades into the future.</code></div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The manufacturing company's warranty provision MUST be discounted under §253(2). The insurer's Deckungsrückstellung, by contrast, is EXPLICITLY EXEMPTED from that same discounting rule under §341e(1) sentence 3 — its own actuarial calculation basis (embedding its own interest-rate assumption directly within the reserving methodology itself) serves this function instead, rather than having the general HGB discounting mechanic applied ON TOP of an already actuarially-derived reserve figure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§341e(2) — Three Specific Technical Provision Categories</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Beyond the specific reserves this Titel names in later paragraphs</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Beitragsüberträge (unearned premium reserves)</strong> — the portion of already-received premiums representing income for a period AFTER the balance sheet date, ensuring premium income is recognised over the genuine coverage period rather than entirely upon receipt</span>
        <span><strong>Rückstellung für Beitragsrückerstattung (RfB)</strong> — a provision for premium refunds, where use is secured by law, statute, business plan, or contract — most relevant to WITH-PROFITS/participating policies where policyholders share in the insurer's own investment or underwriting surplus</span>
        <span><strong>Rückstellung für drohende Verluste</strong> — a provision for threatened losses from insurance business already concluded by year-end, conceptually parallel in SPIRIT to §249's own general onerous-contract provision established very early in this series, but tailored to insurance underwriting specifically</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§341f — the Deckungsrückstellung: Life Insurance's Central Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The single largest liability item on a typical life insurer's balance sheet</div>
      <div class="principle">§341f requires Deckungsrückstellungen (mathematical/actuarial reserves) to be formed for obligations arising from life insurance contracts — representing, in substance, the present value of future policy benefits LESS the present value of future premiums the insurer will still receive. This is genuinely the SINGLE LARGEST liability item on a typical life insurer's own balance sheet, reflecting decades' worth of accumulated policy obligations, calculated using actuarial bases (mortality tables, interest rate assumptions, expense loadings) rather than ordinary accounting measurement.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Illustrating the Deckungsrückstellung Concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A simplified illustration of the underlying actuarial logic</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A simplified whole-life policy reserve</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A life insurer has issued a whole-life policy where the present value of expected future death benefit payments, calculated using the applicable mortality and interest assumptions, is €85,000, and the present value of the future premiums the policyholder will still pay is €32,000.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The Deckungsrückstellung for this policy is APPROXIMATELY €85,000 − €32,000 = <strong>€53,000</strong> — representing the insurer's own NET actuarial liability: what it still owes in EXPECTED future benefits, reduced by what it still expects to COLLECT in future premiums, calculated using the underlying actuarial basis rather than the general HGB provisioning/discounting mechanics established elsewhere in this series.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>The Alterungsrückstellung — German Substitutive Health Insurance's Own Distinctive Reserve</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely distinctive feature of the German private health insurance model</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§341f(3) requires health insurance conducted "nach Art der Lebensversicherung" (in the manner of life insurance — using level, age-independent premiums rather than annually-repriced premiums) to form an Alterungsrückstellung (ageing reserve) as its own Deckungsrückstellung variant — INCLUDING amounts already allocated from the RfB, and write-ups building toward an entitlement to premium reduction in old age. This reflects a genuinely distinctive feature of the German substitutive private health insurance system: policyholders effectively PRE-FUND their own future, higher-age healthcare costs through level premiums paid while younger, with the insurer accumulating this Alterungsrückstellung specifically to smooth premiums across the policyholder's own lifetime, rather than repricing annually as claims experience and age genuinely rise.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>§341g — the Claims Provision (Property/Casualty)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The property/casualty counterpart to life insurance's own Deckungsrückstellung</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§341g requires a Rückstellung für noch nicht abgewickelte Versicherungsfälle (provision for insurance claims that have OCCURRED but are NOT YET fully settled/processed by year-end) — the property/casualty-line equivalent concept to §341f's own life insurance reserve, though addressing a genuinely different underlying uncertainty: not decades-long POLICY obligations, but the SHORTER-term, though still genuinely uncertain, timing and ultimate cost of settling claims already reported (or, in substance, incurred but not yet reported) by the balance sheet date.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>§341h — Schwankungsrückstellung: Smoothing Volatile Claims Years</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A distinctive prudential mechanism, genuinely comparable in spirit to a bank's own general risk reserve</div>
      <div class="principle">§341h requires a Schwankungsrückstellung (equalisation/fluctuation reserve) to be formed "zum Ausgleich der Schwankungen im Schadenverlauf künftiger Jahre" — to offset fluctuations in the CLAIMS EXPERIENCE of FUTURE years, particularly relevant for insurance LINES with genuinely volatile loss experience (certain property lines, credit insurance, hail insurance, and similarly claims-volatile business) where a single unusually bad year could otherwise dramatically distort reported underwriting results. This is conceptually similar in SPIRIT to the credit institution's own §340g Fonds für allgemeine Bankrisiken covered in the previous poster — both are sector-specific mechanisms setting aside amounts in favourable periods specifically to smooth against genuinely foreseeable future volatility.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Worked Example — the Equalization Reserve in Action</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Building the reserve in a good year, drawing on it in a bad one</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A hail-insurance line's own multi-year claims pattern</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A property insurer's hail-insurance line experiences a genuinely mild claims year, well below its long-run average loss experience, followed by a severe hailstorm year with claims substantially exceeding the long-run average.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">In the MILD year, the insurer ALLOCATES an amount to the Schwankungsrückstellung, reflecting the genuinely foreseeable likelihood of future above-average claims years for this inherently volatile line. In the SEVERE year, the insurer DRAWS DOWN the accumulated reserve to help absorb the excess claims cost — smoothing the line's own REPORTED underwriting result across genuinely volatile years, rather than presenting wildly swinging results purely reflecting weather variability from one year to the next.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>§341i–341j — Konzernabschluss Adaptations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The general consolidation framework, carrying the same technical provisions through</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§341i addresses the preparation and deadlines for an insurance group's own Konzernabschluss and Konzernlagebericht, while §341j applies the relevant general provisions correspondingly — ensuring the SAME technical-provisions framework this poster has documented (Deckungsrückstellung, claims provisions, the Schwankungsrückstellung) carries through consistently at the consolidated level, exactly as the previous poster documented for credit institution groups under §340i.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Audit, Disclosure, and Criminal Provisions — By Structural Analogy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Presented with appropriate care, given this poster's own confidence level here</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">This Subsection's own remaining Titel — governing audit, disclosure, and the extension of criminal/administrative provisions — follow, BY STRUCTURAL ANALOGY, the SAME general patterns this poster series documented in detail for credit institutions in the previous poster: adapted audit arrangements (including a comparable rule for insurance undertakings organised as cooperatives), disclosure obligations mirroring the general §325 framework, and criminal-provision extensions to non-corporate-form insurance undertakings paralleling §335b and §340m/n. Given this poster's own primary focus on the well-confirmed, conceptually central technical-provisions cluster, these later Titel are flagged at a structural level rather than reproduced in the same paragraph-by-paragraph detail.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Selecting appropriate actuarial bases (mortality, morbidity, interest, expense assumptions) for the Deckungsrückstellung, given how materially these assumptions drive the reserve's own size</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Estimating claims provisions for genuinely long-tail lines, where the ultimate settlement cost of claims incurred years ago may only become clear much later</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 17)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Arguably the single largest global insurance-accounting convergence effort in decades</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">This Subsection sits at one of the most significant IFRS divergence points anywhere in this poster series: IFRS 17 (effective globally since 2023, following an unusually long and contested development process) requires insurance contracts to be measured using a genuinely different, current-fulfilment-value approach — explicitly discounting future cash flows, incorporating a risk adjustment for non-financial risk, and recognising a "contractual service margin" spreading expected profit over the coverage period — a STRUCTURALLY different model from HGB's own actuarial-basis Deckungsrückstellung and separate Schwankungsrückstellung/RfB mechanisms. A German group using §315e's own EU-IFRS permission for its Konzernabschluss would apply IFRS 17 there, while its INDIVIDUAL HGB accounts continue following this Subsection's own framework in full — potentially the MOST significant dual-framework divergence this entire poster series has documented for a single company.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§341–341p event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Deckungsrückstellung (§341f)</span><span><span class="badge bo">Bilanz</span></span><span>Typically the largest single liability item for a life insurer</span></div>
